<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Gutian rule</h1><p class="st-meta">2154 BCE – 2119 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The period when the Gutians, a mountain people of the Zagros, dominated parts of Mesopotamia after the fall of Akkad, later remembered as an age of chaos.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a> came from the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> and raided the plains as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> weakened. After the death of Shar-kali-sharri around 2154 BCE they took control of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> gives about 21 Gutian kings reigning for around a century, though many reigns were short.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gutian rule was loose and patchy. Some kings, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erridupizir">Erridupizir</a>, left Akkadian inscriptions at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nippur">Nippur</a> imitating Akkadian royal style, but southern cities like <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lagash">Lagash</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> and Ur and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/uruk">Uruk</a> carried on largely independently. Their language is almost unknown. Later Mesopotamian texts, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/curse-of-agade">Curse of Agade</a>, portrayed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a> as barbarians sent by the gods to punish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 2119 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu-hengal">Utu-hengal</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/uruk">Uruk</a> defeated the last Gutian king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirigan">Tirigan</a>, and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> soon founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>. 'Gutian' became a general term for eastern highlanders in later Mesopotamian writing.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gutian-rule.jpg" alt="Gutian rule" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Votive_mace_head_mentioning_the_name_of_La-arab,_king_of_Gutium,_c._2150_BCE,_from_Sippar,_Iraq._British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2154 BCE – 2119 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Erridupizir, La-erabum, Tirigan</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gutian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gutians, Gutian dynasty, Gutium</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2136&amp;focus=gutian-rule" aria-label="Gutian rule on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5784 989 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gutian rule lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0M6193 1305h0M6178 1292h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6104 1245h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6104 1245h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-2136&amp;focus=gutian-rule">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
